\section{Problem and protocol}\label{sec:protocol}
\paragraph{Setting.}
A history matrix $X\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times N}$ holds the scores of $M$ models on $N$ benchmarks with no missing cell (a dense pool; Section~\ref{sec:data}). A new model has an unobserved row $x\in\mathbb{R}^N$. The evaluator picks a set $S$ of $k$ benchmarks, runs the model on them, and predicts its scores on a set of target benchmarks $T$ disjoint from $S$. The selector and the predictor may use $X$ and the observed $x_S$, and nothing else about the new model.

\paragraph{Targets, splits and leakage.}
In the main design a target set $T$ of 30\% of the benchmarks is drawn at random, $S$ is chosen among the other 70\%, and the procedure is repeated over five random partitions per fold; every selector, predictor and $k$ is therefore scored on identical targets. (The literal problem, $T=[N]\setminus S$, lets the targets depend on the selector; it is a secondary design, Appendix~\ref{app:protocol}.) The main split is a grouped 10-fold split that holds out whole model \emph{families}, so no sibling of a test model is in the history; the family is the organisation plus the first two name tokens (on HELM MMLU, the organisation), and organisation-level families are a robustness check. Secondary splits are a random 10-fold split, which shares families between history and test, and a time-forward split that trains on the models released before a cut (55, 70 and 85\% of the release order) and tests on the next block. In every fold, the standardisation (training mean and standard deviation per benchmark), the transform's mask and clipping, the rank and the parameters of the factor model, the selection and the predictor's tuning use only the training rows. The one exception is the choice between the identity and the logit transform, made once per pool by the majority over outer folds of the inner held-out likelihood; both are reported (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:robust}).

\paragraph{Metrics and references.}
Errors are on scores standardised with the training fold's mean and standard deviation: the error of a model is its mean squared error over the target benchmarks, and we report the root of the mean over test models (standardised RMSE) and use the squared version for closure. Three references bracket it. The \emph{column mean} predicts the training mean (floor); the \emph{leave-one-benchmark-out} (LOBO) reference predicts each target from all other $N-1$ benchmarks of the new model (the better, on held-out error, of the factor-model and Ledoit--Wolf conditional predictors, which is Ledoit--Wolf throughout; a reference, not an achievable method); the \emph{in-sample} fit, in which the factor model has seen the test model's targets, is drawn only as a labelled reference. The \emph{closure}
\begin{equation}
C(k)=\frac{E_{\rm colmean}-E_k}{E_{\rm colmean}-E_{\rm LOBO}},\qquad k^*(c)=\min\{k: C(k)\ge c\},
\end{equation}
is the share of the gap between floor and LOBO closed with $k$ benchmarks, and $k^*(c)$ is the smallest $k$ on the grid ($k=1,\dots,10,12,15,20,30$, capped at half the benchmarks) that reaches level $c$; it is censored (``$>G$'') when the grid maximum $G$ does not. Closure is relative to LOBO, not to the noise floor. Uncertainty is a family-clustered bootstrap over families (2\,000 replicates; paired for differences between methods), and random selection is the mean of 100 to 200 draws, so intervals reflect family-level variation but not the choice of the pool.

\paragraph{Pre-specified criteria.}
Four hypotheses and their decision rules were fixed in the study plan before the final runs; the departures from the plan are listed in the released code (Appendix~\ref{app:repro}). H1: $k^*(0.9)$ lies within $[r_{\rm eff},2r_{\rm eff}]$ ``knee near $r_{\rm eff}$'' on most pools, where $r_{\rm eff}$ is the smallest rank within one standard error of the best held-out likelihood; the alternatives are ``dominated by a general factor'' ($k^*<r_{\rm eff}$) and ``above window'' ($k^*>2r_{\rm eff}$ or censored). H2: greedy A-optimal selection beats random selection, with a grid-averaged paired gap in RMSE over $k=1,\dots,\min(10,N/2)$ whose family-bootstrap interval excludes zero and which is at least 10\% of the random error. H3: random-split accuracy overstates accuracy for new families and newer models. H4: the closed-form reducible risk of Section~\ref{sec:theory} is within 15\% (10\% for synthetic data with fitted parameters) of the empirical one. The headline $k^*$ is the smaller of the $k^*$ of the factor model and of the best simple predictor (chosen per fold on training rows), because $k^*$ belongs to the selector and predictor together ; we show both and their intervals, since a minimum of two noisy estimates is biased low.
